1
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:06,230
John Dalton was born in 1766 in
Eaglesfield,

2
00:00:06,380 --> 00:00:08,970
a village in Cumberland in the north-west
of England.

3
00:00:09,764 --> 00:00:11,254
His family were Quakers.

4
00:00:12,304 --> 00:00:13,894
Quakers keep things plain.

5
00:00:14,688 --> 00:00:16,698
Plain clothes, plain words.

6
00:00:17,492 --> 00:00:20,422
Dalton lives that way, and he writes
everything down.

7
00:00:21,216 --> 00:00:25,966
For more than 50 years, he keeps a diary
of the weather, every single day.

8
00:00:27,096 --> 00:00:28,586
He's been teaching in Kendal,

9
00:00:28,636 --> 00:00:31,886
a market town on the edge of the Lake
District, since he was 15.

10
00:00:32,680 --> 00:00:34,710
That's where the geranium happens.

11
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:39,090
Dalton takes it for granted that everyone
will see the same change,

12
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:40,590
so he asks around.

13
00:00:41,640 --> 00:00:45,370
He was surprised, he wrote later, to find
they all agreed.

14
00:00:46,164 --> 00:00:49,234
By candle, the colour was not much
different from daylight.

15
00:00:50,187 --> 00:00:54,797
Except, he says, for his brother, who saw
it in the same light as myself.

16
00:00:55,847 --> 00:00:57,837
There's another story about the two
brothers.

17
00:00:58,631 --> 00:01:02,041
It comes from a biography written 30 years
after John died,

18
00:01:02,111 --> 00:01:03,481
so it may have grown in the telling.

19
00:01:03,531 --> 00:01:09,621
One day in Kendal, John passes a shop
window and sees a pair of stockings.

20
00:01:10,415 --> 00:01:11,725
He buys them for his mother.

21
00:01:12,559 --> 00:01:14,829
To him, they're a sensible dark colour.

22
00:01:15,623 --> 00:01:18,833
A bluish-dark drab, in the biographer's
words.

23
00:01:19,883 --> 00:01:23,693
His mother opens the present and says,
they're as red as a cherry, John!

24
00:01:24,547 --> 00:01:26,017
John can't see it.

25
00:01:26,811 --> 00:01:27,881
Neither can Jonathan.

26
00:01:28,675 --> 00:01:31,225
To both of them, the stockings are dark.

27
00:01:32,275 --> 00:01:37,125
Two grown men, one pair of stockings, and
a mother who is quite sure she's right.

28
00:01:38,595 --> 00:01:40,145
So, look at what stays the same.

29
00:01:40,939 --> 00:01:43,809
Everyone sees the same flower by the same
candle.

30
00:01:44,603 --> 00:01:46,433
Everyone sees the same stockings.

31
00:01:47,227 --> 00:01:49,177
Only the person looking changes.

32
00:01:49,971 --> 00:01:52,501
The odd thing isn't the flower or the
candle.

33
00:01:53,295 --> 00:01:54,665
It's the two brothers.

34
00:01:55,715 --> 00:01:57,145
They aren't rare either.

35
00:01:58,159 --> 00:02:00,469
Today, we'd call this a kind of colour
blindness.

36
00:02:01,262 --> 00:02:03,392
About eight in a hundred men of European
descent

37
00:02:03,442 --> 00:02:06,452
have some trouble telling certain reds and
greens apart.

38
00:02:07,566 --> 00:02:10,656
For women of European descent, it's about
four in a thousand.

39
00:02:11,706 --> 00:02:15,636
So in a room of fifty of those men, about
four would have some form of it.

40
00:02:16,430 --> 00:02:20,200
It's less common among men in China and
Japan, but it's still there.

41
00:02:21,250 --> 00:02:22,700
Picture it from the inside.

42
00:02:23,494 --> 00:02:26,284
You've looked at the world one way your
whole life.

43
00:02:27,078 --> 00:02:30,068
Grass, blood, cherries, sky.

44
00:02:30,978 --> 00:02:33,788
You use the same colour names as everyone
else,

45
00:02:33,858 --> 00:02:36,968
and nobody has ever told you your red is
different.

46
00:02:37,762 --> 00:02:40,032
Nobody else can see what you see.

47
00:02:40,826 --> 00:02:46,496
Then one evening, a flower changes colour,
and almost nobody else sees it happen.

48
00:02:47,706 --> 00:02:49,476
Dalton, though, is a scientist.

49
00:02:50,270 --> 00:02:52,680
It's not enough for him to know that he's
different.

50
00:02:53,474 --> 00:02:54,824
He wants to know why.
